        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Bathroom caulk is the most frequently replaced sealant in any house, and almost always for the same two reasons: the wrong material for a permanently wet joint, or the right material applied over the last one's residue.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="why-it-fails" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Why Bathroom Caulk Fails
        </h2>

        <p>
          A bathroom joint is the hardest environment a sealant faces in an ordinary house, and it is worth understanding why before choosing a product. Three things happen there that happen almost nowhere else.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It is wet, repeatedly, forever.</strong> Not damp — actually wet, several times a day, for years. Most sealants are formulated to shed occasional water rather than to sit in it, and the ones that are not truly waterproof gradually absorb moisture, soften, and lose their grip on the substrate.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The joint moves more than you would think.</strong> An acrylic or fibreglass tub deflects visibly when somebody steps into it. A shower tray does the same. The bead at the perimeter is being stretched and released every single day, and a material without real elasticity simply cannot survive that cycle.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>It is a food source.</strong> Soap residue, shampoo, skin oils and dead skin all accumulate on a horizontal bead in a warm humid room. Mould does not eat silicone, but it very happily eats what settles on it, and the black staining that follows is growing in that surface film rather than in the sealant itself.
        </p>

        <p>
          Understanding those three tells you what to look for: something waterproof rather than water-resistant, something genuinely elastic rather than merely flexible, and something carrying a biocide to slow the colonisation. That combination points almost exclusively at silicone.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Which Type to Buy
        </h2>

        <p>
          The shelf offers three categories, and the packaging does its best to blur the distinction between them.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>100 per cent silicone</strong> is the correct answer for wet joints. It is genuinely waterproof, it stays elastic for many years, and it bonds well to porcelain, acrylic and glazed tile. It cannot be painted, it cannot be tooled with water, and removing it later is real work — all acceptable prices for a joint that lasts.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Siliconised acrylic</strong> — sold under names like "kitchen and bath" or "tub and tile" — is acrylic latex with a small silicone content. It tools easily, cleans up with water, and takes paint. It is also not waterproof in any meaningful sense, and in a shower it will disappoint. Its honest use case is decorative bathroom trim away from direct water.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Elastomeric polymers and hybrids</strong> sit between the two: highly elastic, paintable, and in some cases able to bond to a damp surface. They cost more and are fussier to tool, and they are the right answer for a joint that has already failed repeatedly.
        </p>

        <p>
          The distinction that catches people out is that all three are sold in the same aisle with near-identical marketing. Read the front of the tube for the words "100% silicone" and treat everything else as a decorative product until proven otherwise. Our <a href="/silicone-vs-latex-caulk/">silicone vs latex comparison</a> works through the difference in more detail.
        </p>

        <p>
          One further split within silicone itself: <strong>acetoxy cure</strong> smells of vinegar as it cures and can corrode some metals and etch natural stone, while <strong>neutral cure</strong> does neither. For a bathroom with marble, travertine or exposed metal fixtures, pay the small premium for neutral cure.
        </p>


        <!-- Product Reviews -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
