        <p>
          A note on colour, because it comes up constantly. White is the default and it shows staining first; clear hides discolouration far better but reveals every flaw in the joint behind it, including the ragged grout line most tub edges have. Almond and biscuit exist for older fixtures and are worth matching properly rather than approximating, because a slightly wrong off-white reads as dirt. If the joint underneath is untidy, white is the more forgiving choice despite the staining.
        </p>

        <p>
          On quantity, a single 10 oz cartridge is enough for a standard three-wall alcove tub with material to spare, and one cartridge will not usually stretch to a tub plus a separate shower enclosure. Buy two if you are doing the whole bathroom in one session — an opened tube keeps badly, and stopping halfway to go to the store means the joint you already cleaned sits collecting dust.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-what" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Which Product Goes Where
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tub and Shower Perimeter</h3>
        <p>
          Mildewcide silicone, without exception. This is the joint that moves most and gets wettest, and it is the one that fails first when the wrong product is used. Fill the tub before applying so the bead cures at the joint's widest, and leave the bottom edge where the tub apron meets the floor open as a leak indicator.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shower Corners and Panel Joints</h3>
        <p>
          Neutral-cure silicone. Vertical corners see constant running water and differential movement between panels or between tile fields. This is also where a three-sided bond does the most damage, so the sealant should bridge the corner rather than being pushed deep into it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sink and Vanity Backsplash</h3>
        <p>
          Silicone where water pools, siliconised acrylic where it does not. The joint immediately behind a tap sees standing water and wants silicone; the return along the wall a foot away is decorative and can take a paintable product that matches the trim.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Toilet Base</h3>
        <p>
          Contested, and reasonably so. Sealing the base looks tidier and stops mopping water running underneath, but it also hides a leaking wax ring until the subfloor is soft. The usual compromise is to seal the front and sides and deliberately leave a gap at the back, so a leak still announces itself while the visible joint looks finished.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Around Trim and Baseboards</h3>
        <p>
          Paintable acrylic, and this is the one place in a bathroom where it is clearly the right choice. The joint is cosmetic, it needs to take paint, and silicone would look wrong and reject the finish.
        </p>

        <h2 id="mould" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. The Mould Problem
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every bathroom bead eventually goes black, and it is worth being clear about why so the expectation is realistic. Mould does not digest cured silicone. What it grows on is the film of soap scum, body oils and organic residue that settles on the bead and never fully rinses away, particularly on the horizontal surface at the top of a tub joint.
        </p>

        <p>
          Mildewcide slows this by leaching a biocide slowly to the surface, killing spores before they establish. It works, and a warranted product genuinely stays clean longer than an unwarranted one. But the biocide is finite. Once it is depleted — typically several years in, faster in a room that stays humid — the bead is no more resistant than any other.
        </p>

        <p>
          Which means ventilation does more than product choice. A bathroom that dries out within thirty minutes of a shower is a hostile environment for mould regardless of what is in the joint. One that stays humid for hours will stain any sealant on the market. If your extractor fan is undersized, or vents into a loft rather than outside, fixing that will do more for your caulk than any upgrade in the aisle.
        </p>

        <p>
          Day to day, the useful habit is a quick squeegee or towel wipe of the tub joint after showering. It takes seconds and it removes the residue film that the mould actually needs.
        </p>

        <p>
          One warning worth repeating: if the staining reappears within weeks of a fresh bead, the mould is not on the sealant but in the grout or substrate behind it. Replacing the caulk again will not help. The joint needs opening up, treating and thorough drying before anything new goes in.
        </p>

        <h2 id="doing-it" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Doing the Job Properly
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Remove everything.</strong> Score both edges, pull the bead out, then shave the residue film with a plastic razor. New silicone does not bond to old silicone, and the invisible film left behind is what causes an otherwise perfect job to peel in a strip six months later. Our <a href="/how-to-remove-old-caulk/">caulk removal guide</a> covers the method in detail.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean and dry.</strong> Wipe the joint faces with isopropyl alcohol and let them dry completely. Grout is porous and holds water for hours, so if you have washed the area, wait overnight rather than minutes.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Load the tub.</strong> Fill an acrylic or fibreglass tub with water before applying. It sinks to roughly its in-use position and the bead cures at the joint's widest point rather than being stretched every time it is used.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Mask if you want a crisp line.</strong> Two strips of tape either side of the joint, bead, tool, then pull the tape immediately while the sealant is still wet. Pulling cured tape tears the edge off the bead.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Tool once.</strong> Silicone skins fast and does not accept a second pass. A wetted finger or a shaped tool, one continuous stroke, and then leave it alone even if it is not perfect.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Wait.</strong> Twenty-four hours before any water reaches the joint, forty-eight if the room is cool or badly ventilated. This is the step people compress and it is the one that most reliably ruins the work — the bead cures either way, but without the bond it needed. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">caulk drying and cure times</a> explains what is happening in those hours.
        </p>


        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Replace Rather Than Repair</h3>
        <p>
          Spot repairs have a narrow window of usefulness in a bathroom. If a short section has lifted and the rest of the run is firmly bonded and clean, cutting back to sound material and lapping in a new length works acceptably. In practice that situation is rare, because whatever caused the first section to fail — movement, a wet substrate, residue from a previous bead — usually applies along the whole joint.
        </p>

        <p>
          The signs that say replace the whole run are worth knowing. A bead that lifts away from the tub when you pull at it anywhere along its length has an adhesion problem, not a local one. Staining that runs continuously rather than appearing in patches means the biocide is depleted throughout. And a bead that has gone hard and slightly brittle — press it and it does not spring back — has reached the end of its elastic life and will crack at the next thermal or load cycle regardless of how it looks today.
        </p>

        <p>
          Budget two evenings rather than one. Removal, cleaning and drying on the first, application on the second, and the shower back in service the morning after that. Compressing it into a single evening is exactly how a well-chosen sealant ends up failing early, and it is the most common reason people conclude that bathroom caulk simply does not last.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using painter's acrylic in a shower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It is cheap, it is paintable, and it will not survive constant wetting. The tube says it is for kitchens and baths; the joint disagrees within a year.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Caulking over the old bead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Nothing bonds to cured silicone, including new silicone. The new bead adheres to a failing layer and lifts off with it, looking undamaged.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Recaulking a tub that isn't loaded</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Fill the tub with water before you apply. An empty tub sits higher than a loaded one, so the bead you lay gets stretched every time somebody steps in.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Showering too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Twenty-four hours minimum, forty-eight in a cold or unventilated room. Water at the joint during the cure prevents the bond forming at all.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Treating mould stains as a caulk problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">If the grout behind the bead is colonised, replacing the sealant just gives the same mould a fresh surface. Treat and dry the substrate first.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk for a bathroom?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A 100 per cent silicone sealant with a mildewcide, applied to a properly stripped and dried joint. Silicone is the only common material that stays flexible and genuinely waterproof through years of daily wetting, and the mildewcide buys several extra years before staining starts.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use silicone or acrylic in a bathroom?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Silicone for anything that gets wet — tub perimeters, shower corners, the joint behind a sink. Acrylic latex only for cosmetic trim work away from water, where paintability matters more than waterproofing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my bathroom caulk keep going black?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Mould is feeding on soap residue, body oils and moisture sitting on the bead, and often on colonised grout behind it. Poor ventilation is the usual root cause. A mildewcide slows the process but nothing prevents it in a bathroom that never dries out.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you caulk over old bathroom caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No, not reliably. New silicone does not bond to cured silicone, and any bead applied over an old one inherits the old one's adhesion. The removal is unglamorous but it is what determines how long the new joint lasts.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does bathroom caulk last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A properly installed silicone bead in a well-ventilated bathroom should give eight to ten years before it needs replacing, and staining usually appears well before failure. In a poorly ventilated room, expect two to four.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to fill the tub before caulking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">For an acrylic or fibreglass tub, yes. The weight of water deflects the tub downward to roughly its in-use position, so the bead is installed at the joint's widest. Skip this and the sealant is stretched every time the tub is used.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk for a shower corner?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A neutral-cure silicone. Vertical shower corners see constant water and considerable movement between wall panels, and silicone's elasticity handles both. Neutral cure avoids the acetic acid smell and is safer on natural stone and metal fixtures.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is mould-resistant caulk actually different?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, though the difference is in degree rather than kind. Mildewcide is a biocide dispersed through the sealant that leaches slowly to the surface. It delays colonisation by years rather than preventing it, and it stops working once it is depleted.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Related Caulking Guides
        </h2>

        <ul>
          <li><a href="/silicone-vs-latex-caulk/">Silicone vs Latex Caulk</a> — the decision that matters most in a wet room.</li>
          <li><a href="/how-to-remove-old-caulk/">How to Remove Old Caulk</a> — the preparation that decides whether a new bead lasts.</li>
          <li><a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">How Long Does Caulk Take to Dry</a> — why you cannot shower tonight.</li>
          <li><a href="/caulk-vs-sealant/">Caulk vs Sealant</a> — why the label on the tube is doing a lot of work.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            For almost every bathroom joint the answer is a 100 per cent neutral-cure silicone with a mildewcide, and the specific brand matters far less than getting the old bead completely out first. Product choice is maybe a quarter of the outcome here.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            The other three quarters are preparation and ventilation. A mediocre sealant in a dry, well-ventilated bathroom outlasts the best product on the shelf in a room where the fan does not work.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
